### Games Social Choice

148. **Payoff matrix minimax theorem.**
   Matrix statement: Zero-sum games have equal max-min and min-max values.
   Tournament statement: A tournament sign matrix is an antisymmetric game; mixed equilibria identify balanced cyclic cores.

149. **Replicator dynamics matrix form.**
   Matrix statement: Population shares evolve by payoff differences.
   Tournament statement: Dominance dynamics on a tournament can cycle; equilibria expose nontransitive support.

150. **Condorcet majority matrix.**
   Matrix statement: Pairwise majority margins form a skew matrix.
   Tournament statement: Social choice tournaments should keep margin magnitude, not only win orientation, when proving stability.

151. **Borda score vector.**
   Matrix statement: Row sums of the majority matrix rank alternatives.
   Tournament statement: Borda is the score vector of A or S; it is a first-order shadow and needs cycle sidecars.

152. **McGarvey realization matrix idea.**
   Matrix statement: Any tournament can be realized as a majority relation.
   Tournament statement: Matrix proof: majority tournaments are universal, so social-choice realizability alone gives no structural proof unless voter profile matrices are retained.

153. **Maximal lottery equilibrium.**
   Matrix statement: A skew payoff matrix defines a mixed equilibrium.
   Tournament statement: Balanced support of a tournament game can be a canonical cyclic perspective, complementary to rooted node views.

154. **Pairwise comparison consistency index.**
   Matrix statement: Reciprocal matrices measure inconsistency by eigenvalues.
   Tournament statement: Tournament inconsistency is not noise by default; high inconsistency may be the central cyclic object.

155. **Elo update matrix.**
   Matrix statement: Pairwise outcomes update latent ratings.
   Tournament statement: Elo extracts a ranking pressure channel and discards exact cycle witnesses unless match history is retained.

### Physics Control

156. **Hamiltonian matrix.**
   Matrix statement: Symplectic systems have paired eigenvalues.
   Tournament statement: Edge-cycle coordinates may form Hamiltonian pairs; paired eigenvalues suggest conserved cyclic quantities.

157. **Lindblad superoperator matrix.**
   Matrix statement: Quantum Markov evolution acts on density matrices.
   Tournament statement: Uncertainty over tournament classes can evolve by measurement; decoherence corresponds to forgetting labels.

158. **Scattering S-matrix unitarity.**
   Matrix statement: Incoming and outgoing amplitudes preserve probability.
   Tournament statement: A proof quotient should conserve obligation mass: every incoming residual exits as certificate, descent, annihilation, or named debt.

159. **Green function resolvent.**
   Matrix statement: Poles of (zI-M)^{-1} identify modes.
   Tournament statement: Tournament resolvent poles reveal persistent cycles or bottlenecks in proof-state dynamics.

160. **Controllability Gramian.**
   Matrix statement: Reachability energy is a PSD matrix.
   Tournament statement: Edge flips or sidecar additions with low controllability energy are efficient proof moves.

161. **Observability Gramian.**
   Matrix statement: Output energy detects hidden states.
   Tournament statement: A scalar invariant set is proof-safe only if its observability Gramian detects route/status-changing coordinates.

162. **Algebraic Riccati equation.**
   Matrix statement: Optimal feedback solves a matrix quadratic equation.
   Tournament statement: Adaptive tournament querying should balance comparison cost against reduction in cyclic uncertainty.

163. **Transfer matrix method for Ising models.**
   Matrix statement: Partition functions are traces/products of local matrices.
   Tournament statement: Layered tournament families can be counted by transfer matrices with weights for cycles, owners, or endpoints.

164. **Kasteleyn orientation matrix.**
   Matrix statement: Planar dimer counts are Pfaffians.
   Tournament statement: Planar subcarriers inside tournaments may have Pfaffian certificates even when the whole tournament is nonplanar.

165. **Dirac gamma matrices.**
   Matrix statement: Clifford generators anticommute.
   Tournament statement: Small parity-rich tournament signs can be represented by Clifford generators; anticommutation tracks cycle parity.

